1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,734
Sweetheart, when my mother died,

2
00:00:02,734 --> 00:00:05,383
she left me her little house on

3
00:00:05,383 --> 00:00:08,033
Elm Street, the house I grew up

4
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:08,289
in.

5
00:00:08,469 --> 00:00:10,826
My children wanted me to sell it.

6
00:00:11,006 --> 00:00:14,106
It was worth about ninety thousand dollars.

7
00:00:14,286 --> 00:00:16,245
Instead, on Christmas Eve,

8
00:00:16,245 --> 00:00:18,582
I handed the keys to a homeless

9
00:00:18,582 --> 00:00:20,692
family I had met in a church

10
00:00:20,692 --> 00:00:23,028
parking lot three weeks before.

11
00:00:23,208 --> 00:00:25,454
My son called me a foolish old

12
00:00:25,454 --> 00:00:27,850
woman and didn't speak to me for

13
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:28,374
months.

14
00:00:28,724 --> 00:00:31,138
Tonight I'm going to tell you how

15
00:00:31,138 --> 00:00:32,454
I met that family,

16
00:00:32,454 --> 00:00:35,526
what my children said at Christmas dinner,

17
00:00:35,526 --> 00:00:38,159
and what that house looked like when

18
00:00:38,159 --> 00:00:40,353
my son finally came to see it,

19
00:00:40,353 --> 00:00:41,670
three years later.

20
00:00:41,850 --> 00:00:42,782
Stay with me,

21
00:00:42,782 --> 00:00:45,581
honey, because my son said something on

22
00:00:45,581 --> 00:00:48,236
that front porch that I've written in

23
00:00:48,236 --> 00:00:49,814
the front of my Bible.

24
00:00:50,163 --> 00:00:51,687
My mother, Dorothy,

25
00:00:51,687 --> 00:00:54,493
lived in that little white house on

26
00:00:54,493 --> 00:00:56,979
Elm Street for sixty-one years.

27
00:00:57,159 --> 00:00:59,094
It's where I learned to walk,

28
00:00:59,094 --> 00:01:01,296
where Danny and I fought over the

29
00:01:01,296 --> 00:01:03,832
bathroom, where my father hung his hat

30
00:01:03,832 --> 00:01:05,634
every night at six o'clock.

31
00:01:05,814 --> 00:01:08,604
When she passed in nineteen eighty-nine,

32
00:01:08,604 --> 00:01:10,905
the house stood empty for a year,

33
00:01:10,905 --> 00:01:13,765
honey, because I couldn't bring myself to

34
00:01:13,765 --> 00:01:15,160
do anything with it.

35
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:18,061
I'd go sit in her kitchen sometimes,

36
00:01:18,061 --> 00:01:19,345
just to smell it.

37
00:01:19,696 --> 00:01:21,978
My children were grown by then,

38
00:01:21,978 --> 00:01:24,996
with houses and mortgages and children of

39
00:01:24,996 --> 00:01:25,733
their own.

40
00:01:25,913 --> 00:01:28,873
And they had plans for Grandma's house.

41
00:01:29,053 --> 00:01:30,690
Sell it, they said.

42
00:01:30,870 --> 00:01:32,832
Ninety thousand dollars.

43
00:01:33,012 --> 00:01:35,410
Split it, and it would pay off

44
00:01:35,410 --> 00:01:36,689
Mark's car loan,

45
00:01:36,689 --> 00:01:38,687
and Susan's credit cards,

46
00:01:38,687 --> 00:01:42,683
and maybe start the grandchildren's college funds.

47
00:01:42,863 --> 00:01:44,231
They weren't wrong,

48
00:01:44,231 --> 00:01:45,023
sweetheart.

49
00:01:45,203 --> 00:01:46,619
It made sense.

50
00:01:46,799 --> 00:01:48,754
In a minute I'll tell you why

51
00:01:48,754 --> 00:01:49,899
I couldn't do it.

52
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:52,119
Harold left it up to me.

53
00:01:52,299 --> 00:01:53,644
He said, Ruthie,

54
00:01:53,644 --> 00:01:55,747
it's your mother's house.

55
00:01:55,927 --> 00:01:57,450
Whatever you decide,

56
00:01:57,450 --> 00:01:58,516
I'll back you.

57
00:01:58,696 --> 00:02:00,584
And I prayed about it,

58
00:02:00,584 --> 00:02:01,099
honey.

59
00:02:01,279 --> 00:02:02,997
I really did.

60
00:02:03,177 --> 00:02:04,567
For a whole year,

61
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:05,302
I prayed.

62
00:02:05,482 --> 00:02:08,010
And the answer came in the strangest

63
00:02:08,010 --> 00:02:08,431
place.

64
00:02:08,611 --> 00:02:11,258
In the parking lot of First Methodist,

65
00:02:11,258 --> 00:02:13,557
on a Wednesday night in December,

66
00:02:13,557 --> 00:02:14,880
at the soup supper.

67
00:02:15,231 --> 00:02:17,196
Every Wednesday in winter,

68
00:02:17,196 --> 00:02:19,919
our church served a free soup supper

69
00:02:19,919 --> 00:02:21,128
in the basement.

70
00:02:21,308 --> 00:02:22,841
I ladled the soup.

71
00:02:23,021 --> 00:02:24,096
And one night,

72
00:02:24,096 --> 00:02:26,092
I noticed a car in the far

73
00:02:26,092 --> 00:02:28,087
corner of the parking lot,

74
00:02:28,087 --> 00:02:30,160
an old brown station wagon,

75
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,926
with fogged-up windows.

76
00:02:32,106 --> 00:02:34,846
It was there the next Wednesday too.

77
00:02:35,026 --> 00:02:36,210
And the next.

78
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:37,994
And the third Wednesday,

79
00:02:37,994 --> 00:02:39,664
I took a bowl of soup out

80
00:02:39,664 --> 00:02:41,535
to it in the snow and tapped

81
00:02:41,535 --> 00:02:42,404
on the glass.

82
00:02:42,754 --> 00:02:45,224
Inside were five people,

83
00:02:45,224 --> 00:02:45,842
honey.

84
00:02:46,022 --> 00:02:47,448
A man named Luis,

85
00:02:47,448 --> 00:02:48,622
his wife Rosa,

86
00:02:48,622 --> 00:02:50,216
and three children,

87
00:02:50,216 --> 00:02:52,145
the littlest about two,

88
00:02:52,145 --> 00:02:54,914
all bundled up in blankets in the

89
00:02:54,914 --> 00:02:55,333
back.

90
00:02:55,513 --> 00:02:57,989
They'd been living in that car for

91
00:02:57,989 --> 00:02:58,717
six weeks.

92
00:02:58,897 --> 00:03:01,582
Luis had worked at a factory in

93
00:03:01,582 --> 00:03:03,228
Toledo that closed.

94
00:03:03,408 --> 00:03:06,196
They'd come to Mansfield because a cousin

95
00:03:06,196 --> 00:03:07,488
promised him a job,

96
00:03:07,488 --> 00:03:09,188
and the job fell through,

97
00:03:09,188 --> 00:03:11,024
and the cousin disappeared.

98
00:03:11,204 --> 00:03:12,864
And their money ran out.

99
00:03:13,215 --> 00:03:15,870
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

100
00:03:15,870 --> 00:03:17,918
tap the like button for me,

101
00:03:17,918 --> 00:03:18,752
sweetheart.

102
00:03:18,932 --> 00:03:22,102
It helps more people find these stories.

103
00:03:22,282 --> 00:03:24,824
I asked Rosa where they went when

104
00:03:24,824 --> 00:03:26,287
it got really cold.

105
00:03:26,467 --> 00:03:28,787
She said, we run the engine for

106
00:03:28,787 --> 00:03:30,733
twenty minutes every hour,

107
00:03:30,733 --> 00:03:32,005
when we have gas.

108
00:03:32,185 --> 00:03:33,671
And we pray.

109
00:03:33,851 --> 00:03:36,313
The two-year-old was coughing.

110
00:03:36,493 --> 00:03:38,815
That deep, rattly cough.

111
00:03:38,995 --> 00:03:41,401
I stood there in the snow holding

112
00:03:41,401 --> 00:03:42,494
that soup bowl,

113
00:03:42,494 --> 00:03:45,191
and I thought about my mother's empty

114
00:03:45,191 --> 00:03:47,087
house, eleven blocks away.

115
00:03:47,437 --> 00:03:50,188
That night, I couldn't sleep,

116
00:03:50,188 --> 00:03:50,757
honey.

117
00:03:50,937 --> 00:03:53,175
I lay awake listening to the wind

118
00:03:53,175 --> 00:03:55,209
and thinking about that cough.

119
00:03:55,389 --> 00:03:57,030
At two in the morning,

120
00:03:57,030 --> 00:03:57,702
I got up,

121
00:03:57,702 --> 00:03:58,821
put on my coat,

122
00:03:58,821 --> 00:04:01,506
and drove back to the church parking

123
00:04:01,506 --> 00:04:03,818
lot with a thermos of hot cocoa

124
00:04:03,818 --> 00:04:06,802
and every blanket from our linen closet.

125
00:04:06,982 --> 00:04:08,909
The windows were dark.

126
00:04:09,089 --> 00:04:10,729
I left it all on the hood

127
00:04:10,729 --> 00:04:11,581
of their car,

128
00:04:11,581 --> 00:04:13,024
with a note that said,

129
00:04:13,024 --> 00:04:13,942
from a friend.

130
00:04:14,293 --> 00:04:16,641
I didn't say anything to them that

131
00:04:16,641 --> 00:04:17,056
night.

132
00:04:17,236 --> 00:04:19,297
I went home and told Harold,

133
00:04:19,297 --> 00:04:20,181
and he said,

134
00:04:20,181 --> 00:04:22,832
Ruthie, I've been waiting a year for

135
00:04:22,832 --> 00:04:24,820
you to know what to do with

136
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:25,630
that house.

137
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:27,540
I think you know now.

138
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:29,270
So the next morning,

139
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:30,898
I went to Elm Street,

140
00:04:30,898 --> 00:04:32,836
and I turned the heat on,

141
00:04:32,836 --> 00:04:34,929
and I scrubbed the kitchen.

142
00:04:35,109 --> 00:04:38,127
The ladies from church brought sheets and

143
00:04:38,127 --> 00:04:39,672
towels and groceries.

144
00:04:39,852 --> 00:04:42,639
The men's group fixed the furnace.

145
00:04:42,989 --> 00:04:44,240
On Christmas Eve,

146
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:46,743
I drove to the church parking lot,

147
00:04:46,743 --> 00:04:49,540
and I tapped on that fogged-up window,

148
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:51,454
and I handed Rosa a set of

149
00:04:51,454 --> 00:04:53,884
keys on my mother's old key ring,

150
00:04:53,884 --> 00:04:56,166
with a little brass bell on it.

151
00:04:56,346 --> 00:04:58,520
I said, there's a house on Elm

152
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:01,419
Street that's been waiting for a family.

153
00:05:01,599 --> 00:05:02,586
It's warm.

154
00:05:02,766 --> 00:05:05,088
There's a turkey in the icebox.

155
00:05:05,268 --> 00:05:06,894
And it's yours.

156
00:05:07,074 --> 00:05:09,768
Rosa looked at those keys and didn't

157
00:05:09,768 --> 00:05:10,591
understand.

158
00:05:10,942 --> 00:05:12,603
We drove them there,

159
00:05:12,603 --> 00:05:13,101
honey.

160
00:05:13,281 --> 00:05:16,050
When Luis walked through that front door,

161
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:18,346
into my mother's warm living room,

162
00:05:18,346 --> 00:05:20,980
with a little Christmas tree the church

163
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:23,682
youth group had decorated in the corner,

164
00:05:23,682 --> 00:05:25,505
he got down on his knees on

165
00:05:25,505 --> 00:05:26,586
my mother's rug.

166
00:05:26,766 --> 00:05:28,647
Just went right down.

167
00:05:28,827 --> 00:05:31,390
The children ran from room to room

168
00:05:31,390 --> 00:05:33,575
screaming, there's a bathtub!

169
00:05:33,755 --> 00:05:35,334
There's a bathtub!

170
00:05:35,514 --> 00:05:38,886
Rosa held the two-year-old and cried and

171
00:05:38,886 --> 00:05:39,392
cried.

172
00:05:39,572 --> 00:05:41,473
And Luis's oldest boy,

173
00:05:41,473 --> 00:05:42,597
who was nine,

174
00:05:42,597 --> 00:05:44,844
came and stood next to me,

175
00:05:44,844 --> 00:05:46,314
and took my hand,

176
00:05:46,314 --> 00:05:48,388
and said very seriously,

177
00:05:48,388 --> 00:05:51,240
ma'am, I'll take good care of it.

178
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:52,790
I promise.

179
00:05:52,970 --> 00:05:54,142
And he did,

180
00:05:54,142 --> 00:05:54,781
honey.

181
00:05:54,961 --> 00:05:56,424
For thirty years.

182
00:05:56,774 --> 00:05:59,203
That first night in the house,

183
00:05:59,203 --> 00:06:00,742
Rosa told me later,

184
00:06:00,742 --> 00:06:03,333
none of them could sleep either.

185
00:06:03,513 --> 00:06:06,002
The children kept getting up to turn

186
00:06:06,002 --> 00:06:08,076
the bathroom light on and off,

187
00:06:08,076 --> 00:06:09,666
and run the warm water,

188
00:06:09,666 --> 00:06:11,048
just to see it work.

189
00:06:11,228 --> 00:06:12,295
And at midnight,

190
00:06:12,295 --> 00:06:14,430
Luis went out on the front porch

191
00:06:14,430 --> 00:06:16,498
in the snow and stood there for

192
00:06:16,498 --> 00:06:18,566
an hour, looking at the street.

193
00:06:18,746 --> 00:06:21,068
She asked him what he was doing.

194
00:06:21,248 --> 00:06:24,104
He said, I'm making sure it's real.

195
00:06:24,454 --> 00:06:26,360
So far, the house,

196
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:28,266
the car, the soup,

197
00:06:28,266 --> 00:06:29,643
and the keys.

198
00:06:29,823 --> 00:06:31,829
The next day was Christmas,

199
00:06:31,829 --> 00:06:34,207
and my children came for dinner,

200
00:06:34,207 --> 00:06:35,396
and I told them.

201
00:06:35,576 --> 00:06:37,635
I thought they'd understand,

202
00:06:37,635 --> 00:06:38,444
sweetheart.

203
00:06:38,624 --> 00:06:39,564
They didn't.

204
00:06:39,744 --> 00:06:42,197
My son Mark set down his fork

205
00:06:42,197 --> 00:06:43,380
and said, Mom,

206
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:46,594
you gave Grandma's house to strangers?

207
00:06:46,774 --> 00:06:48,864
To people you met in a parking

208
00:06:48,864 --> 00:06:49,142
lot?

209
00:06:49,322 --> 00:06:51,518
Do you know how much that house

210
00:06:51,518 --> 00:06:52,155
is worth?

211
00:06:52,506 --> 00:06:54,312
And then it got worse,

212
00:06:54,312 --> 00:06:54,804
honey.

213
00:06:54,984 --> 00:06:57,422
Mark said, they'll trash it.

214
00:06:57,602 --> 00:06:59,565
They'll sell everything in it.

215
00:06:59,745 --> 00:07:01,999
People like that don't take care of

216
00:07:01,999 --> 00:07:02,450
things.

217
00:07:02,630 --> 00:07:04,608
You're a foolish old woman,

218
00:07:04,608 --> 00:07:06,879
and Dad shouldn't have let you.

219
00:07:07,059 --> 00:07:08,864
And he got up from the table

220
00:07:08,864 --> 00:07:11,120
and took his family home before the

221
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:11,378
pie.

222
00:07:11,558 --> 00:07:13,137
Susan didn't yell.

223
00:07:13,317 --> 00:07:14,460
But she said,

224
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:17,450
Mom, that was our inheritance too.

225
00:07:17,630 --> 00:07:19,441
You should have asked us.

226
00:07:19,621 --> 00:07:21,467
And that one hurt more.

227
00:07:21,817 --> 00:07:23,486
I want to be fair to my

228
00:07:23,486 --> 00:07:25,009
children, sweetheart.

229
00:07:25,189 --> 00:07:27,035
They weren't greedy people.

230
00:07:27,215 --> 00:07:28,853
They were tired people,

231
00:07:28,853 --> 00:07:31,630
with bills, who'd been counting on that

232
00:07:31,630 --> 00:07:32,057
money.

233
00:07:32,237 --> 00:07:34,744
And maybe I should have asked them.

234
00:07:34,924 --> 00:07:36,782
I've thought about that a lot.

235
00:07:36,962 --> 00:07:38,901
But I knew what they'd say.

236
00:07:39,081 --> 00:07:42,085
And I knew a two-year-old was coughing

237
00:07:42,085 --> 00:07:42,796
in a car.

238
00:07:42,976 --> 00:07:45,414
Sometimes you can't wait for a vote.

239
00:07:45,765 --> 00:07:48,735
I didn't actually give them the house

240
00:07:48,735 --> 00:07:49,939
that first day,

241
00:07:49,939 --> 00:07:50,420
honey.

242
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:52,489
I rented it to them for one

243
00:07:52,489 --> 00:07:53,468
dollar a year.

244
00:07:53,648 --> 00:07:56,551
Harold's idea, so it was all legal

245
00:07:56,551 --> 00:07:57,491
and proper.

246
00:07:57,671 --> 00:07:58,990
And I told Luis,

247
00:07:58,990 --> 00:08:01,134
when you get on your feet,

248
00:08:01,134 --> 00:08:03,278
we'll talk about the rest.

249
00:08:03,458 --> 00:08:06,250
Luis found work in January at a

250
00:08:06,250 --> 00:08:07,150
body shop.

251
00:08:07,330 --> 00:08:09,768
Rosa started cleaning houses,

252
00:08:09,768 --> 00:08:11,870
and then at the hospital.

253
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:15,004
The children went to Lincoln Elementary,

254
00:08:15,004 --> 00:08:17,147
where I used to knit mittens.

255
00:08:17,497 --> 00:08:19,619
And here's what they did with my

256
00:08:19,619 --> 00:08:21,409
mother's house, sweetheart.

257
00:08:21,589 --> 00:08:24,396
Luis painted the whole outside,

258
00:08:24,396 --> 00:08:26,932
white, the way it was when I

259
00:08:26,932 --> 00:08:27,928
was a girl.

260
00:08:28,108 --> 00:08:30,586
He fixed the porch steps my father

261
00:08:30,586 --> 00:08:32,044
always meant to fix.

262
00:08:32,224 --> 00:08:36,058
Rosa planted my mother's rose bushes again,

263
00:08:36,058 --> 00:08:38,911
red ones, along the front fence.

264
00:08:39,091 --> 00:08:42,197
Every spring, they sent me a picture

265
00:08:42,197 --> 00:08:43,492
of those roses.

266
00:08:43,672 --> 00:08:45,197
And every Christmas,

267
00:08:45,197 --> 00:08:48,248
the youngest delivered a plate of Rosa's

268
00:08:48,248 --> 00:08:49,697
tamales to my door.

269
00:08:50,047 --> 00:08:51,430
And the neighbors,

270
00:08:51,430 --> 00:08:52,276
sweetheart.

271
00:08:52,456 --> 00:08:55,207
At first, some of them weren't happy.

272
00:08:55,387 --> 00:08:57,583
One man on Elm Street called the

273
00:08:57,583 --> 00:09:00,396
city twice to complain about strangers in

274
00:09:00,396 --> 00:09:01,494
Dorothy's house.

275
00:09:01,674 --> 00:09:03,445
But that first spring,

276
00:09:03,445 --> 00:09:06,422
Luis shoveled every walk on the block

277
00:09:06,422 --> 00:09:08,756
before dawn after a big snow.

278
00:09:08,936 --> 00:09:11,471
Rosa took soup to the old widower

279
00:09:11,471 --> 00:09:13,929
next door when he had pneumonia.

280
00:09:14,109 --> 00:09:17,343
By summer, that same man who'd complained

281
00:09:17,343 --> 00:09:19,867
was teaching Luis's boy to fish.

282
00:09:20,217 --> 00:09:22,954
Mark didn't come to that house for

283
00:09:22,954 --> 00:09:23,920
three years.

284
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:26,375
He came to my house at Christmas,

285
00:09:26,375 --> 00:09:28,649
but things were stiff between us,

286
00:09:28,649 --> 00:09:30,648
and we never talked about it.

287
00:09:30,828 --> 00:09:32,589
Then, in the third year,

288
00:09:32,589 --> 00:09:34,718
his own company laid him off.

289
00:09:34,898 --> 00:09:36,631
Twenty-two years, honey,

290
00:09:36,631 --> 00:09:38,724
and they let him go two weeks

291
00:09:38,724 --> 00:09:40,169
before Thanksgiving.

292
00:09:40,349 --> 00:09:42,505
He came to my house white as

293
00:09:42,505 --> 00:09:43,815
a sheet and said,

294
00:09:43,815 --> 00:09:46,049
Mom, I don't know what to do.

295
00:09:46,229 --> 00:09:48,121
We might lose the house.

296
00:09:48,472 --> 00:09:51,786
Somehow, sweetheart, the whole town knew within

297
00:09:51,786 --> 00:09:52,280
a week.

298
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:54,712
And the first person to show up

299
00:09:54,712 --> 00:09:57,472
at Mark's door wasn't anybody from his

300
00:09:57,472 --> 00:09:58,925
church, or his work,

301
00:09:58,925 --> 00:10:00,378
or his neighborhood.

302
00:10:00,558 --> 00:10:01,870
It was Luis.

303
00:10:02,050 --> 00:10:04,361
With a casserole from Rosa,

304
00:10:04,361 --> 00:10:05,730
and a job offer.

305
00:10:05,910 --> 00:10:08,205
He'd saved enough to buy the body

306
00:10:08,205 --> 00:10:09,665
shop where he worked,

307
00:10:09,665 --> 00:10:11,890
and he needed a manager who knew

308
00:10:11,890 --> 00:10:12,446
numbers.

309
00:10:12,626 --> 00:10:15,765
Mark had been an accountant for twenty-two

310
00:10:15,765 --> 00:10:16,214
years.

311
00:10:16,564 --> 00:10:18,179
That Christmas Eve,

312
00:10:18,179 --> 00:10:20,644
Mark asked me to drive him to

313
00:10:20,644 --> 00:10:21,579
Elm Street.

314
00:10:21,759 --> 00:10:24,581
He hadn't seen the house since Grandma

315
00:10:24,581 --> 00:10:24,952
died.

316
00:10:25,132 --> 00:10:26,151
We pulled up,

317
00:10:26,151 --> 00:10:28,661
and he looked at the fresh white

318
00:10:28,661 --> 00:10:30,778
paint, and the fixed steps,

319
00:10:30,778 --> 00:10:33,209
and the red roses on the fence,

320
00:10:33,209 --> 00:10:34,385
buried in snow.

321
00:10:34,565 --> 00:10:37,127
And Luis's children ran out on the

322
00:10:37,127 --> 00:10:38,106
porch waving.

323
00:10:38,286 --> 00:10:40,221
And my son sat in my car

324
00:10:40,221 --> 00:10:42,477
and cried like a little boy.

325
00:10:42,828 --> 00:10:44,999
Then he walked up onto that porch,

326
00:10:44,999 --> 00:10:46,340
and he looked around,

327
00:10:46,340 --> 00:10:48,511
and he said the thing I've written

328
00:10:48,511 --> 00:10:50,107
in the front of my Bible.

329
00:10:50,287 --> 00:10:51,419
He said, Mom,

330
00:10:51,419 --> 00:10:53,422
I said they'd trash it.

331
00:10:53,602 --> 00:10:56,366
They made it more Grandma's house than

332
00:10:56,366 --> 00:10:58,548
it ever was when it was empty.

333
00:10:58,728 --> 00:11:00,829
I was the one who was poor.

334
00:11:01,009 --> 00:11:02,751
I just had more money.

335
00:11:02,931 --> 00:11:05,441
And then he hugged Luis on that

336
00:11:05,441 --> 00:11:08,842
porch, and they've been like brothers ever

337
00:11:08,842 --> 00:11:09,328
since.

338
00:11:09,678 --> 00:11:12,392
Luis and Rosa bought that house from

339
00:11:12,392 --> 00:11:13,749
me for one dollar,

340
00:11:13,749 --> 00:11:15,785
honey, the fifth Christmas.

341
00:11:15,965 --> 00:11:18,095
I signed the deed at my kitchen

342
00:11:18,095 --> 00:11:18,507
table.

343
00:11:18,687 --> 00:11:21,109
Today, their oldest son is a nurse,

344
00:11:21,109 --> 00:11:23,877
and their daughter teaches second grade,

345
00:11:23,877 --> 00:11:26,160
and the little one with the cough

346
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:27,337
is in law school.

347
00:11:27,517 --> 00:11:30,733
And Mark still manages that body shop.

348
00:11:30,913 --> 00:11:33,100
He says it's the best job he

349
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:33,803
ever had.

350
00:11:34,154 --> 00:11:36,742
Here's what I want you to remember,

351
00:11:36,742 --> 00:11:37,555
sweetheart.

352
00:11:37,735 --> 00:11:40,708
An empty house isn't an inheritance.

353
00:11:40,888 --> 00:11:42,374
A family is.

354
00:11:42,554 --> 00:11:44,367
Tell me in the comments,

355
00:11:44,367 --> 00:11:47,162
honey, would you have given away that

356
00:11:47,162 --> 00:11:47,616
house?

357
00:11:47,796 --> 00:11:49,758
Be honest with Grandma.

358
00:11:49,938 --> 00:11:52,250
There are more of my family's stories

359
00:11:52,250 --> 00:11:54,438
in the playlist right there on your

360
00:11:54,438 --> 00:11:56,376
screen, and I'll see you in the

361
00:11:56,376 --> 00:11:56,939
next one.

362
00:11:57,119 --> 00:11:58,024
Good night.

363
00:11:58,204 --> 00:11:59,713
Merry Christmas.
